## BUG-05: PBFT protocol does not enforce primary — premature advancement
# Severity: MEDIUM
# Files: consensus/PBFT.hpp:219-221
#
# Classic PBFT requires primary (view % N) to send PRE_PREPARE. This impl
# allows ANY node to send PRE_PREPARE. Receiving any single PRE_PREPARE
# advances state IDLE→PREPARE immediately (line 219-221):
#   if (msg.stage_str == "PRE_PREPARE" && round.state == PBFTStage::IDLE)
#       round.state = PBFTStage::PREPARE;
# The node then broadcasts its own PREPARE (line 1100). With quorum=3 (n=5),
# attacker casting one vote needs only 2 honest relays to reach COMMIT.
#
# Additionally, initiate_consensus() at line 1170 accepts any target + any
# evidence from any source — no validation that evidence corresponds to a
# real anomaly. The only guard is 30s cooldown (rate, not authenticity).
#
# Fix: Rename protocol to "Byzantine Voting" or implement proper primary
# selection where only the expected primary's PRE_PREPARE is accepted.

## BUG-06: Missing per-source rate limiting on discovery/telemetry socket
# Severity: MEDIUM
# Files: consensus/MeshNode.cpp:452-472
#
# The discovery socket drain loop processes ALL pending messages in a tight
# loop without per-source-IP rate limiting. Each message triggers string
# splitting, base64 decode, and Ed25519 signature verification. An attacker
# on LAN can saturate CPU with forged UDP datagrams (~20K forged pkts/sec
# at ~50μs/verify = 100% CPU).
#
# By contrast, the consensus socket goes through PeerManager::check_rate_limit
# at line 977. Discovery/telemetry bypass this entirely.
#
# Fix: Add per-source-IP rate limiting to the drain loop, or use recvfrom +
# per-IP credit system.

## BUG-07: StateJournal log rotation has TOCTOU race — silent data loss
# Severity: MEDIUM
# Files: common/StateJournal.hpp:106-119
#
# During log rotation (>10MB), StateJournal:
# 1. ::lseek(m_fd, 0, SEEK_SET) — dead code, misleading
# 2. ::rename(m_path, backup) — closes fd, releases POSIX lock
# 3. ::close(m_fd) + ::open(...) — window where another writer can interleave
#
# During the rename→close→open window, the POSIX advisory lock is released
# (closed fd releases all locks). A concurrent thread or process can write
# to the path during this window, losing entries.
#
# Fix: Remove dead lseek(). Hold write lock during rename using a separate
# fd, or use copy-truncate (preserves lock, non-atomic but safe).

## BUG-08: XDP interface detection assumes eth0/lo — silent fallback
# Severity: MEDIUM
# Files: cell/NodeAgent.cpp:62-77
#
# XDP attachment tries only two hardcoded interfaces: eth0 and lo.
# Cloud/K8s deployments use ens5, enp0s1, bond0, etc. If neither exists,
# XDP enforcement silently falls back to nftables/iptables with only a
# log warning.
#
# Fix: Iterate /sys/class/net/* to find ALL interfaces, or accept
# NEURO_XDP_IFACE env var for explicit configuration.

## BUG-09: UDP broadcast timing jitter (10-80ms) adds latency to consensus
# Severity: MEDIUM
# Files: consensus/MeshNode.cpp:291-294
#
# send_udp_broadcast() adds random sleep 10-80ms before every send:
#   static thread_local std::mt19937 gen{std::random_device{}()};
#   std::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(10, 80);
#   std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(dis(gen)));
#
# Same for send_udp_discovery (5-50ms) and send_udp_unicast (15-100ms).
# In a 5-node PBFT round, each message incurs this delay before broadcast.
# A full PRE_PREPARE→PREPARE→COMMIT→EXECUTED cycle adds 30-240ms of
# artificial latency per node. Intent is "timing obfuscation" but the
# cost to consensus convergence time is significant.
#
# Fix: Make jitter configurable (env var) or document that this intentionally
# slows consensus to prevent timing side-channel attacks. Default to 0.

